<!-- the Riemann sum calculator used in this Preview is due to Peter Keep -->
<!-- see Peter's original work at https://beta.doenet.org/activityViewer/anvfo1993TKgun9i4sWeLM -->
<!-- a rough draft of this ACS Preview was completed by Jacob Levin after the 2024 Doenet workshop -->
<!-- Significantly rewritten by Chrissy Safranski in Spring 2026 -->

<setup>
  <!-- Calculate the offset: sample point x_i^* = x_i + offset -->
  <!-- Left, Mid and Right Endpoint Rule corresponds to 0, 1, 2 in following computation -->

  <number name="offset">$dxN*($ruleChoice.selectedIndex-1) / 2</number>
  <!-- use a numeric version of dx in computations, but compute a symbolic version if it's desired to display the dx to the user. -->
  <math simplify="true" name="dx">($b-$a)/$n</math>
  <number name="dxN">$dx</number>

  <function name="f">$fnFormula</function>

  <!-- Compute the x_i's here, instead of within the graph -->
  <sequence from="$a" to="$b-$dxN" step="$dxN" name="xiValues" />

  <group name="group">$xiValues</group>
  <!-- I'm not sure why using the above seems to help with indices not existing when the value of n changes, but it seems to? -->

  <!-- compute the minimum y and maximum y values for the range on the graph -->
  <function name="fforrange" domain="($a,$b)">$f</function>
  <min name="minY">$fforrange.minimumValues 0 $$f($a) $$f($b)</min>
  <max name="maxY">$fforrange.maximumValues 0 $$f($a) $$f($b)</max>


<number name="RiemannSum" displaydecimals="6">
  <sum>
    <mathList name="fValues"><repeat for="$group" valueName="xVal">
      <number>$$f($xVal+$offset)</number>
    </repeat></mathList>
  </sum>*$dxN
  </number>
  <!-- Using $xiValues instead of $group leads to weird warnings when the value of n changes. -->
  <!-- Seems like multiplying once by $dxN at the end instead of inside every computation might help when the n is large.  -->

  <boolean name="smalln">
    <isBetween limits="1 29">$n</isBetween> and <isInteger>$n</isInteger>
  </boolean>
  <boolean name="largen">
    $n>29 and <isInteger>$n</isInteger>
  </boolean>

  <isInteger name="nisInt">$n</isInteger>

</setup>

<p>
  The graph provided below includes a Riemann sum calculator: in the boxed windows, you can input your own function <m>f(x)</m>, your own interval from <m>x=a</m> to <m>x=b</m>, and the number of rectangles, <m>n</m>, you'd like to use.  Then, from the drop-down menu choose your strategy for selecting the <m>x</m>-values from each of the subintervals (a Left, Midpoint, or Right Riemann sum).  The resulting value of the Riemann sum will be computed and displayed below the graph.
</p>
<p>
  <alert>Note:</alert> you have to hit the <q>enter</q> key, or click outside of the box, after anything you change in one of these boxes in order to update the resulting computations.  Computations involving larger <m>n</m> values can take several seconds to appear.
</p>

<!-- Start by definining the function, a, b, and n (and hence dx), and rule choice -->
<tabular>
    <row>
      <cell halign="right"><m>f(x) = </m><mathInput prefill="x^2+1" name="fnFormula" /></cell>
      <cell halign="right"><m>a =</m><mathInput name="a" prefill="0" /></cell>
      <cell halign="right"><m>b =</m><mathInput name="b" prefill="2" /></cell>
      <cell halign="right"><m>n =</m><mathInput prefill="6" name="n" /></cell>
      <!--<cell halign="right"><m>\Delta x = <math simplify="true" name="dx">($b-$a)/$n</math></m></cell>-->
    </row>
</tabular>
<tabular>
    <row>
      <cell halign="left">choice for <m>x_k^*</m>:<choiceInput name="ruleChoice" inline="true" preselectChoice="1">
          <choice>Left Riemann Sum</choice>
          <choice>Midpoint Riemann Sum</choice>
          <choice>Right Riemann Sum</choice>
      </choiceInput>
      </cell>
    </row>
</tabular>

<!-- Graph the function, points at (a,0) and (b,0), and then the n rectangles -->
<graph size="small" xmin="$a-1.5" xmax="$b+1.5" ymin="$minY-1" ymax="$maxY+1" identicalAxisScales="false" showNavigation="false" fixAxes>
  $f
  <regionBetweenCurveXAxis function="$f" boundaryValues="$a $b" />

  <conditionalContent>
    <case condition="$smalln">
      <repeatForSequence from="1" to="$n" step="1" valueName="index">
        <rectangle stylenumber="6" filled="true" draggable="false"
          vertices="($xiValues[$index],0)
                    ($xiValues[$index]+$dxN, $fValues[$index])" />
      </repeatForSequence>
    </case>
    <case condition="$largen">
      <sequence from="$a" to="$b-($b-$a)/30" step="($b-$a)/30" name="xiValueslargen" hide/>
      <repeat for="$xiValueslargen" valueName="xVal" name="largenrectangles">
        <rectangle stylenumber="6" filled="true" draggable="false"
          vertices="($xVal,0)
                    ($xVal+($b-$a)/30, $$f($xVal))" />
      </repeat>
    </case>
  </conditionalContent>
</graph>

<p>
  <conditionalContent>
    <case condition="$nisInt">
      The value of the Riemann sum is:
      <conditionalContent>
        <case condition="$ruleChoice.selectedIndex=1">
          <m>L_{$n}=</m>
        </case>
        <case condition="$ruleChoice.selectedIndex=3">
          <m>R_{$n}=</m>
        </case>
        <case condition="$ruleChoice.selectedIndex=2">
          <m>M_{$n}=</m>
        </case>
      </conditionalContent>
      $RiemannSum
    </case>
    <else>The value of n needs to be an integer</else>
  </conditionalContent>
</p>

<aside startOpen><title>(a)</title>

<p>
  In the boxes provided above the graph, update the entries to compute a left Riemann sum with <m>n = 5</m> subintervals for the function <m>f(x) = 2x+1</m> on the interval <m>[1,4]</m>.  Once you do, you'll see the value of <m>L_5</m> populate in the table below automatically.
</p>

  <p>
    Then, for this same function and interval, <m>f(x) = 2x+1</m> on <m>[1,4]</m>, continue to use the boxes above to compute each of <math>L_n, M_n, R_n</math> for <math> n = 5, n = 25</math>, and <math>n = 100</math> so that all of the values in the table below are filled in.  Click the "Check Work" button to get a score for having done so.
  </p>

  <updateValue
  triggerWhen="$fnFormula = 2x+1 and $a=1 and $b=4 and $n=5 and $ruleChoice.selectedIndex=1"
  target="$linearL5"
  newValue="$RiemannSum"
/>

  <updateValue
  triggerWhen="$fnFormula = 2x+1 and $a=1 and $b=4 and $n=5 and $ruleChoice.selectedIndex=2"
  target="$linearM5"
  newValue="$RiemannSum"
/>

  <updateValue
  triggerWhen="$fnFormula = 2x+1 and $a=1 and $b=4 and $n=5 and $ruleChoice.selectedIndex=3"
  target="$linearR5"
  newValue="$RiemannSum"
/>

  <updateValue
  triggerWhen="$fnFormula = 2x+1 and $a=1 and $b=4 and $n=25 and $ruleChoice.selectedIndex=1"
  target="$linearL25"
  newValue="$RiemannSum"
/>

  <updateValue
  triggerWhen="$fnFormula = 2x+1 and $a=1 and $b=4 and $n=25 and $ruleChoice.selectedIndex=2"
  target="$linearM25"
  newValue="$RiemannSum"
/>

  <updateValue
  triggerWhen="$fnFormula = 2x+1 and $a=1 and $b=4 and $n=25 and $ruleChoice.selectedIndex=3"
  target="$linearR25"
  newValue="$RiemannSum"
/>

  <updateValue
  triggerWhen="$fnFormula = 2x+1 and $a=1 and $b=4 and $n=100 and $ruleChoice.selectedIndex=1"
  target="$linearL100"
  newValue="$RiemannSum"
/>

  <updateValue
  triggerWhen="$fnFormula = 2x+1 and $a=1 and $b=4 and $n=100 and $ruleChoice.selectedIndex=2"
  target="$linearM100"
  newValue="$RiemannSum"
/>

  <updateValue
  triggerWhen="$fnFormula = 2x+1 and $a=1 and $b=4 and $n=100 and $ruleChoice.selectedIndex=3"
  target="$linearR100"
  newValue="$RiemannSum"
/>


  <tabular top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
    <row bottom="minor">
      <cell right="minor"><m>n</m></cell>
      <cell right="minor"><math>L_n</math></cell>
      <cell right="minor"><math>M_n</math></cell>
      <cell right="minor"><math>R_n</math></cell>
    </row>

    <row>

      <cell right="minor">5</cell>

      <cell right="minor">
        <m>L_5 =</m><mathInput name="linearL5" disabled forAnswer="$lineartable"/>
      </cell>

      <cell>
        <m>M_5=</m> <mathInput name="linearM5" disabled forAnswer="$lineartable"/>
      </cell>

      <cell>
        <m>R_5=</m> <mathInput name="linearR5" disabled forAnswer="$lineartable"/>
      </cell>

    </row>

    <row>

    <cell right="minor">25</cell>

      <cell right="minor">
        <m>L_{25} =</m><mathInput name="linearL25" disabled forAnswer="$lineartable"/>
      </cell>

      <cell>
        <m>M_{25}=</m> <mathInput name="linearM25" disabled forAnswer="$lineartable"/>
      </cell>

      <cell>
        <m>R_{25}=</m> <mathInput name="linearR25" disabled forAnswer="$lineartable"/>
      </cell>


  </row>

  <row>

      <cell right="minor"><m>100</m></cell>

    <cell right="minor">
        <m>L_{100} =</m><mathInput name="linearL100" disabled forAnswer="$lineartable"/>
      </cell>

      <cell>
        <m>M_{100}=</m> <mathInput name="linearM100" disabled forAnswer="$lineartable"/>
      </cell>

      <cell>
        <m>R_{100}=</m> <mathInput name="linearR100" disabled forAnswer="$lineartable"/>
      </cell>

    </row>
  </tabular>
  <answer matchPartial name="lineartable" colorCorrectness="false"><award><when>
    $linearL5=16.2 and $linearM5=18 and $linearR5=19.8 and
    $linearL25=17.64 and $linearM25=18 and $linearR25=18.36 and
    $linearL100=17.91 and $linearM100=18 and $linearR100=18.09
  </when></award></answer>

</aside>
<aside><title>(b)</title>
<p>
  Observe that the region bounded by <m>f(x) = 2x+1</m> and the <m>x</m>-axis on <m>[1,4]</m> is a trapezoid. The area of a trapezoid is <m>\frac{1}{2}(b_1+b_2)h</m>. Find the values of <m>b_1, b_2, h</m>, and the exact area, <m>A</m> of this trapezoid.
</p>
   <p>
    <answer><mathInput name="b1"><label><m>b_1=</m></label></mathInput><mathInput name="b2"><label><m>b_2=</m></label></mathInput>
    <award><when>$b1=3 and $b2=9</when></award>
    <award><when>$b2=3 and $b1=9</when></award>
    </answer>
     and <answer><label><m>h=</m></label>3</answer>, which means that
  </p>

  <p>
    <m>A = </m> <answer>18</answer>
  </p>
</aside>

<aside><title>(c)</title>
  <p>
    Based on your work in (a) and (b), complete the following sentences to summarize what we can observe as we increase the number of subintervals used in the Riemann sum.
  </p>

  <p>
    Because <m>f(x) = 2x+1</m> is linear, each midpoint sum <m>M_n</m> results in <choiceInput name="c1" inline>
      <choice>?</choice>
      <choice>an underestimate of</choice>
      <choice>the exact value of</choice>
      <choice>an overestimate of</choice>
    </choiceInput> the true area, <m>A</m>, bounded by <m>f(x)</m> on the interval <m>[1,4]</m>.  In addition, while each left Riemann sum <m>L_n</m> is <choiceInput name="c2" inline>
      <choice>?</choice>
      <choice>an underestimate of</choice>
      <choice>the exact value of</choice>
      <choice>an overestimate of</choice>
    </choiceInput> the true area, <m>A</m>, and each right Riemann sum <m>R_n</m> is <choiceInput name="c3" inline>
      <choice>?</choice>
      <choice>an underestimate of</choice>
      <choice>the exact value of</choice>
      <choice>an overestimate of</choice>
    </choiceInput> the true area, <m>A</m>, as we increase the value of <m>n</m>, we see that the values of both <m>L_n</m> and <m>R_n</m> <choiceInput name="c4" inline>
      <choice>?</choice>
      <choice>stay the same distance away from</choice>
      <choice>get closer and closer to</choice>
      <choice>change without an obvious pattern relative to</choice>
    </choiceInput> the true area, <m>A</m>.
  </p>
<p>
  <answer><award><when>
    $c1.selectedIndices=3 and $c2.selectedIndices=2 and $c3.selectedIndices=4 and $c4.selectedIndices=3
  </when></award></answer>
</p>

</aside>
<aside><title>(d)</title>

  <p>
    Use the box above the graph to update the function to be <math>f(x) = x^2 + 1</math>, still using the interval <math>[1, 4]</math>. Use the interactive to compute <math>L_n, M_n, R_n</math> for <math> n = 5, n = 25</math>, and <math>n = 100</math> for <math>f(x) = x^2 + 1</math> on <math>[1, 4]</math>.
  </p>
  <p>
     The values in the table below populate automatically as you use the interactive to compute them. Click the "Check Work" button to get a score for having done so.
</p>

   <updateValue
  triggerWhen="$fnFormula = x^2+1 and $a=1 and $b=4 and $n=5 and $ruleChoice.selectedIndex=1"
  target="$quadraticL5"
  newValue="$RiemannSum"
/>

  <updateValue
  triggerWhen="$fnFormula = x^2+1 and $a=1 and $b=4 and $n=5 and $ruleChoice.selectedIndex=2"
  target="$quadraticM5"
  newValue="$RiemannSum"
/>

  <updateValue
  triggerWhen="$fnFormula = x^2+1 and $a=1 and $b=4 and $n=5 and $ruleChoice.selectedIndex=3"
  target="$quadraticR5"
  newValue="$RiemannSum"
/>

  <updateValue
  triggerWhen="$fnFormula = x^2+1 and $a=1 and $b=4 and $n=25 and $ruleChoice.selectedIndex=1"
  target="$quadraticL25"
  newValue="$RiemannSum"
/>

  <updateValue
  triggerWhen="$fnFormula = x^2+1 and $a=1 and $b=4 and $n=25 and $ruleChoice.selectedIndex=2"
  target="$quadraticM25"
  newValue="$RiemannSum"
/>

  <updateValue
  triggerWhen="$fnFormula = x^2+1 and $a=1 and $b=4 and $n=25 and $ruleChoice.selectedIndex=3"
  target="$quadraticR25"
  newValue="$RiemannSum"
/>

  <updateValue
  triggerWhen="$fnFormula = x^2+1 and $a=1 and $b=4 and $n=100 and $ruleChoice.selectedIndex=1"
  target="$quadraticL100"
  newValue="$RiemannSum"
/>

  <updateValue
  triggerWhen="$fnFormula = x^2+1 and $a=1 and $b=4 and $n=100 and $ruleChoice.selectedIndex=2"
  target="$quadraticM100"
  newValue="$RiemannSum"
/>

  <updateValue
  triggerWhen="$fnFormula = x^2+1 and $a=1 and $b=4 and $n=100 and $ruleChoice.selectedIndex=3"
  target="$quadraticR100"
  newValue="$RiemannSum"
/>

  <tabular top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
    <row bottom="minor">
      <cell right="minor"><m>n</m></cell>
      <cell right="minor"><math>L_n</math></cell>
      <cell right="minor"><math>M_n</math></cell>
      <cell right="minor"><math>R_n</math></cell>
    </row>

    <row>

      <cell right="minor">5</cell>

      <cell right="minor">
        <m>L_5 =</m><mathInput name="quadraticL5" disabled forAnswer="$quadratictable"/>
      </cell>

      <cell>
        <m>M_5=</m> <mathInput name="quadraticM5" disabled forAnswer="$quadratictable"/>
      </cell>

      <cell>
        <m>R_5=</m> <mathInput name="quadraticR5" disabled forAnswer="$quadratictable"/>
      </cell>

    </row>

    <row>

    <cell right="minor">25</cell>

      <cell right="minor">
        <m>L_{25} =</m><mathInput name="quadraticL25" disabled forAnswer="$quadratictable"/>
      </cell>

      <cell>
        <m>M_{25}=</m> <mathInput name="quadraticM25" disabled forAnswer="$quadratictable"/>
      </cell>

      <cell>
        <m>R_{25}=</m> <mathInput name="quadraticR25" disabled forAnswer="$quadratictable"/>
      </cell>


  </row>

  <row>

      <cell right="minor"><m>100</m></cell>

    <cell right="minor">
        <m>L_{100} =</m><mathInput name="quadraticL100" disabled forAnswer="$quadratictable"/>
      </cell>

      <cell>
        <m>M_{100}=</m> <mathInput name="quadraticM100" disabled forAnswer="$quadratictable"/>
      </cell>

      <cell>
        <m>R_{100}=</m> <mathInput name="quadraticR100" disabled forAnswer="$quadratictable"/>
      </cell>

    </row>
  </tabular>
  <answer matchPartial name="quadratictable" colorCorrectness="false"><award><when>
    $quadraticL5=19.68 and $quadraticM5=23.91 and $quadraticR5=28.68 and
    $quadraticL25=23.1072 and $quadraticM25=23.9964 and $quadraticR25=24.9072 and
    $quadraticL100=23.77545 and $quadraticM100=23.999775 and $quadraticR100=24.22545
  </when></award></answer>


  <p>
    What do you conjecture is the exact area, <m>A</m> bounded by <math>f(x) = x^2 + 1</math> and the <m>x</m>-axis on <math>[1,4]</math>?
  </p>
  <p>
    <m>A = </m><answer>24</answer>
  </p>

</aside>

<aside><title>(e)</title>

  <p>
    Why can we not compute the exact value of the area bounded by <math>f(x) = x^2 + 1</math> and the <m>x</m>-axis on <math>[1,4]</math> using a geometric formula like we did in (b)?  Complete the following sentence to explain
  </p>
  <p>
    Since <m>f(x) = x^2 + 1</m> is <choiceInput name="e1" inline>
      <choice>?</choice>
      <choice>linear</choice>
      <choice>non-linear</choice>
    </choiceInput> and its curve does not bound a familiar geometric shape (such as a circle), there <choiceInput name="e2" inline>
      <choice>?</choice>
      <choice>is</choice>
      <choice>is not</choice>
    </choiceInput> a known geometric formula to find the area, and thus our best option is to <choiceInput name="e3" inline>
      <choice>?</choice>
      <choice>estimate the area</choice>
      <choice>determine the area exactly</choice>
    </choiceInput>
    by using Riemann sums.
  </p>
  <p>
    <answer><award><when>$e1.selectedIndices=3 and $e2.selectedIndices=3 and $e3.selectedIndices=2</when></award></answer>
  </p>

</aside>
